 <exercises xml:id="hw-hyper-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



<exercise xml:id="ex-cookie-jar-hypergeometric-expanded">
    <title>Discrete Distributions: The Cookie Jar Selection</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A cookie jar currently holds <m>21</m> total cookies: <m>13</m> are chocolate-chip cookies, and <m>8</m> are brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies. You pick <m>3</m> cookies at random from the jar without looking.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part 1: Explaining WHY it is Hypergeometric -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-rv-why-hypergeometric-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let the random variable <m>X</m> represent the number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies found in your sample. This scenario is modeled using a <term>Hypergeometric distribution</term>. Which of the following best explains why the Hypergeometric model applies here?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    Because we are sampling a fixed number of items from a finite population containing two distinct categories without replacement, meaning individual trial outcomes are dependent.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! A hypergeometric random variable tracks a fixed sample size drawn from a small finite population divided into two groups. Because cookies are eaten or set aside (sampling <term>without replacement</term>), the probability of drawing a specific cookie type shifts dynamically with each draw, making the choices dependent.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because each draw is independent, and the probability of picking a brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookie remains perfectly constant at <m>\frac{8}{21}</m> for all three selections.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Constant probabilities and trial independence only hold true if you put each cookie back into the jar before selecting the next one (sampling with replacement), which fits a Binomial distribution.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because we are tracking the infinite number of attempts required to pull our very first edible chocolate-chip cookie.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Counting the number of independent trials required until a success occurs describes a Geometric distribution, not a Hypergeometric distribution.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because the cookies are randomly distributed over a continuous geometric interval of volume or surface area.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Continuous intervals track Poisson patterns or geometric density functions, not discrete group partitions.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: Probability of None -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-prob-none-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that you get none of the brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
        <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.215"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! We compute the combination footprints for the total sample space and successful subset partitions:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>Total ways to pick any 3 cookies out of 21: <me>\binom{21}{3} = \frac{21 \times 20 \times 19}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 1330</me></p></li>
                    <li><p>Ways to choose 0 brussels-sprout cookies (meaning all 3 are chocolate-chip): <me>\binom{8}{0} \times \binom{13}{3} = 1 \times \frac{13 \times 12 \times 11}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 286</me></p></li>
                </ul>
                Dividing the favorable outcomes by the sample space yields:
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{286}{1330} = \frac{143}{665} \approx 0.215</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>

        <hint>
            <p>
                Use combinations to find the ratio: <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{8}{0} \times \binom{13}{3}}{\binom{21}{3}}</me> Find how many ways to pick 3 cookies exclusively from the 13 chocolate-chip options, and divide by the total ways to draw any 3 cookies from the jar.
            </p>
        </hint>

    </task>

    <!-- Part 3: Probability of At Most One -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-prob-at-most-one-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the probability that your sample contains at most one (<m>X \le 1</m>) brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookie. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
               <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.684"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! <p>
                "At most one" means we calculate the combined probabilities for <m>X = 0</m> and <m>X = 1</m>:
                <me>P(X \le 1) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                1. Calculate the probability of choosing exactly 1 brussels-sprout cookie and 2 chocolate-chip cookies:
                <me>P(X = 1) = \frac{\binom{8}{1} \times \binom{13}{2}}{\binom{21}{3}} = \frac{8 \times 78}{1330} = \frac{624}{1330} \approx 0.469</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. Sum the two discrete probabilities together:
                <me>P(X \le 1) = \frac{286}{1330} + \frac{624}{1330} = \frac{910}{1330} = \frac{455}{665} = \frac{13}{19} \approx 0.684</me>
                Thus, the cumulative probability of drawing at most one brussels-sprout cookie is <m>0.684</m>.
            </p>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>
        <hint>
            <p>
                To find <m>P(X \le 1)</m>, calculate the sum of the two disjoint cases: <m>P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)</m>. You found <m>P(X = 0) = \frac{286}{1330}</m> in the previous part. Now evaluate <m>P(X = 1) = \frac{\binom{8}{1} \times \binom{13}{2}}{\binom{21}{3}}</m> and combine them.
            </p>
        </hint>

    </task>

    <!-- Part 4: Expected Value & Interpretation (5-Option Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the expected number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies in your sample, <m>E(X)</m>, and select its correct long-term interpretation.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <m>E(X) \approx 1.143</m>. If you repeatedly sample 3 cookies from this jar over many days, you will draw an average of about 1.143 brussels-sprout cookies per sample.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! For a hypergeometric distribution, the expected value formula is <m>E(X) = n \left(\frac{r}{N}\right)</m>, where <m>n=3</m> is the sample size, <m>r=8</m> is the number of target items (brussels-sprout cookies), and <m>N=21</m> is the total population. This yields <m>3 \times \left(\frac{8}{21}\right) = \frac{24}{21} = \frac{8}{7} \approx 1.143</m>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>E(X) \approx 1.857</m>. If you repeatedly sample 3 cookies from this jar over many days, you will draw an average of about 1.857 brussels-sprout cookies per sample.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This calculation uses the wrong category pool in the numerator, solving for the expected number of <term>chocolate-chip</term> cookies instead: <m>3 \times \left(\frac{13}{21}\right) \approx 1.857</m>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>E(X) = 1.143</m>. In any given single sample of 3 cookies, you are guaranteed to get exactly 1 full brussels-sprout cookie and a fraction of another.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. The value is mathematically right, but the interpretation is wrong. Expected values represent long-run averages over repeated trials; a single discrete draw can only yield integer values (0, 1, 2, or 3 cookies), never a fractional decimal amount.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>E(X) = 0.381</m>. If you repeatedly sample 3 cookies from this jar over many days, you will draw an average of about 0.381 brussels-sprout cookies per sample.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                      Incorrect. This is the simple ratio of brussels-sprout cookies in the jar (\frac{8}{21} \approx 0.381). You forgot to multiply by the sample size multiplier (n = 3).
                      </p>
</feedback>
</choice>
<choice>
  <statement>
    <p>
      E(X) = 1.500. If you repeatedly sample 3 cookies from this jar over many days, you will draw an average of exactly 1.500 brussels-sprout cookies per sample.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <feedback>
    <p>
      Incorrect. This is a blind guess assuming the two cookie types split the sample capacity perfectly in half (3/2 = 1.5), which ignores the actual proportions of cookies in the jar.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>      
</choices>     
</task>           
        </exercise>      




<exercise label="ex-hypergeometric-aerospace-assembly" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Hypergeometric Distributions: Sampling Without Replacement in Precision Assembly</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An aerospace cleanroom facility assembles tracking  modules onto satellite chassis blocks. A plug-and-play module with properly drilled alignment holes takes exactly 1 minute to secure. However, if the mounting holes were improperly drilled at the supplier facility, the technicians must manually re-drill the bracket, stretching the installation time out to 10 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
            The staging inventory area holds a total batch of 20 modules in stock, of which exactly 2 contain improperly drilled alignment holes. A technician randomly selects a subset of 5 tracking modules from the 20 available items to install on the next 5 satellites on the floor. 
        </p>
       
        <p>
            Note: Enter all final probability and timing answers as decimals rounded to exactly three decimal places.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability all 5 fit perfectly -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-perfect-fit-probability">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability that all 5 randomly selected tracking modules fit properly on the first attempt, requiring zero re-drilling work.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>P(X = 0)</m> = <fillin answer="0.553" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate <m>P(X = 0)</m>, use the standard Hypergeometric probability mass function (PMF). We need to choose exactly 0 defective modules from the 2 available, and exactly 5 flawless modules from the 18 working items, divided by the total combinations of drawing 5 cards from a pool of 20:
                <me>P(X = k) = \frac{\binom{M}{k}\binom{N-M}{n-k}}{\binom{N}{n}}</me>
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{2}{0}\binom{18}{5}}{\binom{20}{5}} = \frac{1 \cdot 8568}{15504} = \frac{8568}{15504} \approx 0.55263</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the probability that all 5 modules install cleanly with zero re-drilling is exactly <strong>0.553</strong> (a 55.3% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Mean Time to Install -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-mean-time">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Let the  random variable <m>T</m> represent the total combined time (in minutes) required to complete the installation of all 5 selected tracking modules. Find the expected mean and standard deviation for the installation time.
            </p>
            <p>
                Expected Mean Time = <fillin  answer="9.500" width="8" name="mean_install"/> minutes
                Standard Deviation = <fillin  answer="5.394" width="8" name= "sd_install"/> minutes
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate submit="mean_install">
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
             <evaluate submit="sd_install">
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, calculate the expected number of defective units <m>E(X)</m> pulled inside a standard Hypergeometric draft:
                <me>E(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) = 5 \cdot \left(\frac{2}{20}\right) = 5 \cdot 0.10 = 0.50 \text{ expected defective modules}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Next, define the linear time function, <m>T</m>. Each of the <m>X</m> defective units takes 10 minutes, while each of the remaining <m>(5 - X)</m> working units takes 1 minute:
                <me>T = 10X + 1(5 - X) = 9X + 5</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                By the linear properties of expected value models, apply the expectation transformation:
                <me>E(T) = E(9X + 5) = 9E(X) + 5 = 9(0.50) + 5 = 4.50 + 5 = 9.50</me>
                The expected baseline time to finish the entire assembly sequence is exactly <strong>9.500</strong> minutes.
            </p>
       
   
            <p>
                To evaluate the spread parameter of our timing distribution, we first find the variance of our underlying Hypergeometric counting variable <m>\text{Var}(X)</m>, which incorporates the finite population correction fraction:
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{N-n}{N-1}\right)</me>
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = 5 \cdot (0.10) \cdot (0.90) \cdot \left(\frac{20-5}{20-1}\right) = 0.45 \cdot \left(\frac{15}{19}\right) = \frac{6.75}{19} \approx 0.355263</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Now, scale this variance parameter up to match our linear time transformation matrix <m>T = 9X + 5</m>. Remember that additive constant displacements disappear under variance operations, while coefficient weights grow quadratically:
                <me>\text{Var}(T) = \text{Var}(9X + 5) = 9^2 \cdot \text{Var}(X) = 81 \cdot 0.355263 \approx 28.776315</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Take the square root of the system variance to isolate the final standard deviation performance metric:
                <me>\sigma_T = \sqrt{\text{Var}(T)} = \sqrt{28.776315} \approx 5.36435</me>
                Let us re-verify using exact fractions to confirm precision limits:
                <me>\sigma_T = \sqrt{81 \cdot \frac{27}{76}} = \sqrt{\frac{2187}{76}} = \sqrt{28.7763} \approx 5.36435</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the standard deviation for the total satellite component assembly operation is exactly <strong>5.364</strong> minutes.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

        <!-- Part D: Time Threshold Inequality Probability (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-time-threshold-probability">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <strong>Part D: Downside Schedule Risk Assessment</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                A project manager flags any assembly block sequence that takes strictly longer than 10 minutes to complete, as it threatens to bottleneck the downstream cleanroom satellite alignment schedule.
            </p>
            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that the installation of the 5 chosen modules takes longer than 10 minutes.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>P(T \gt 10)</m> = <fillin answer="0.447" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate the schedule risk parameter, we translate the timing constraint inequality condition <m>T \gt 10</m> back into an equivalent condition for our underlying discrete counting variable <m>X</m> (the number of improperly drilled modules selected).
            </p>
            <p>
                Using the linear time transformation formula derived in Part B, <m>T = 9X + 5</m>:
                <me>T \gt 10 \implies 9X + 5 \gt 10 \implies 9X \gt 5 \implies X \gt \frac{5}{9} \approx 0.556</me>
                Because <m>X</m> is a discrete counting variable that can only take on whole integer values (<m>X \in \{0, 1, 2\}</m>), the condition <m>X \gt 0.556</m> is mathematically equivalent to stating that <m>X \ge 1</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                We can compute <m>P(X \ge 1)</m> efficiently by applying the **Complement Rule**:
                <me>P(T \gt 10) = P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</me>
                Leveraging the baseline clean-fit probability calculated in Part A (<m>P(X = 0) = \frac{8568}{15504} \approx 0.55263</m>):
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - \frac{8568}{15504} = \frac{6936}{15504} \approx 0.44737</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the final probability that the assembly process takes strictly longer than 10 minutes is exactly <strong>0.447</strong> (or a 44.7% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-hypergeometric-student-rentals" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Hypergeometric Distributions: Student Equipment Rentals</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            At the beginning of the semester, a campus student housing agency prepares a batch of used dorm mini-fridges that were returned by departing students, cleaned, and processed to go back out on lease agreements. Because major mechanical overhauls are not performed, a portion of the inventory remains prone to malfunctioning. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Among a specific cluster of eight mini-fridges available today, exactly three are malfunctioning. A student group arrives and needs to lease four mini-fridges. So, four of the eight available  are randomly selected and leased to the students.
        </p>
        
        <p>
           Enter all answers as decimals. Round probabilities to exactly four decimal places. Round expected values and standard deviations to exactly two decimal places.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability of at least one malfunctioning -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-at-least-one">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that the students receive at least one malfunctioning mini-fridge.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin mode="string" answer="0.9286" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We track a total population of <m>N = 8</m> fridges, containing <m>M = 3</m> defective units and <m>N - M = 5</m> flawless units. A sample size of <m>n = 4</m> is drawn without replacement. To find <m>P(X \ge 1)</m>, it is most efficient to apply the **Complement Rule**:
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluate the probability of drawing absolutely zero malfunctioning appliances using the Hypergeometric probability mass function formula, <m>P(X = k) = \frac{\binom{M}{k}\binom{N-M}{n-k}}{\binom{N}{n}}</m>:
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{3}{0}\binom{5}{4}}{\binom{8}{4}} = \frac{1 \cdot 5}{70} = \frac{5}{70} = \frac{1}{14} \approx 0.0714</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Completing the subtraction from certainty:
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - \frac{1}{14} = \frac{13}{14} \approx 0.92857</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the probability is exactly <strong>0.9286</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Probability of exactly two malfunctioning -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-exactly-two">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Find the probability that the students receive exactly two malfunctioning mini fridges.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.4286" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate the probability of hitting exactly two malfunctions, we configure the Hypergeometric PMF at <m>k = 2</m>:
                <me>P(X = 2) = \frac{\binom{3}{2}\binom{5}{2}}{\binom{8}{4}} = \frac{3 \cdot 10}{70} = \frac{30}{70} = \frac{3}{7} \approx 0.42857</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the probability is exactly <strong>0.4286</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Expected Value -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Calculate the expected number of malfunctioning appliances the group of students will get. 
            </p>
            <p>
                Expected Value = <fillin answer="1.50" width="8"/> malfunctioning fridges
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For a Hypergeometric variable, the long-run expectation scales simply as the sample size multiplied by the underlying defective ratio of the population pool:
                <me>E(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) = 4 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}\right) = \frac{12}{8} = 1.50</me>
                The expected average is exactly <strong>1.50</strong> malfunctioning appliances.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Standard Deviation -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-standard-deviation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Compute the standard deviation for the expected number of malfunctioning appliances.
            </p>
            <p>
                Standard Deviation = <fillin  answer="0.73" width="8"/> malfunctioning fridges
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To calculate the standard deviation, we evaluate the Hypergeometric variance model, which incorporates the finite population correction adjustment fraction, <m>\frac{N-n}{N-1}</m>:
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{N-n}{N-1}\right)</me>
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = 4 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{5}{8}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{8-4}{8-1}\right) = 1.5 \cdot 0.625 \cdot \left(\frac{4}{7}\right) = \frac{15}{28} \approx 0.535714</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Take the square root of the variance to isolate the final standard deviation performance metric:
                <me>\sigma_X = \sqrt{\frac{15}{28}} \approx 0.73192</me>
                Rounded to two decimal places, the standard deviation is exactly <strong>0.73</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</exercise>


   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-hyper-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>


   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="binomial-approximation-mixed">
    <title>Binomial Approximation to the Hypergeometric Distribution</title>
    
    <p>
        Recall that the binomial distribution is used when we want to calculate the likelihood that an event happens a certain number of times during <m>n</m> independent Bernoulli trials. The binomial distribution can be used to approximate the hypergeometric distribution if the sample size, <m>n</m>, is small relative to the population size <m>N</m> (e.g., the sample is at most 5% of the population, meaning <m>\frac{n}{N} \le 0.05</m>). To understand how and why this works, answer the questions that follow.
    </p>

    <exercise label="ex-fish-hypergeometric">
        <title>Capture-Recapture Scenario [Revisited]</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose there are 1,000 fish in a lake. During the first week, 65 fish are captured, tagged, and released. Then, 30 fish are captured two weeks later. Let <m>X</m> be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <!-- Interactive short answer for calculation -->
        <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            	Let <m>X</m> be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30. What is the probability that 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged? In other words, use the hypergeometric probability distribution to find <m>P(X=3)</m>.  
              Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three places. 
            </p>
            <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.184"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the hypergeometric formula:
                <me>P(X=3) = \frac{\binom{65}{3}\binom{935}{27}}{\binom{1000}{30}} \approx 0.184</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
</task>

    <task> 
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the capture of each fish to be a trial. If the sample is taken without replacement, then we have 30 trials.  Compute each of the following probabilities. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places. Remember - The sample of size 30 is taken <em>without replacement</em>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
        <!-- Part B1: First fish -->
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>What is the probability that the first fish captured is tagged?
                </p> 
                <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.0650"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
