    <!-- Non-interactive conceptual explanations using essay text boxes -->
   <task>
     <statement correct="yes">
    <p>
      These trials can be considered Bernoulli trials.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Write.
    </p>
  </feedback>

    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain why our random variable <m>X</m> (number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 60) is NOT a binomial random variable. Also tell me, What changes would you have to make to the physical capture-recapture protocol in order for <m>X</m> to be a true binomial random variable?
            </p>
        </statement>
         <response/>
        <solution>
            <p>
                It is not binomial because true binomial distribution requires completely independent trials with a constant probability of success. To make it a true binomial setup, you would have to sample <strong>with replacement</strong>: throw each fish back into the lake immediately after checking it before drawing the next one.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Because the sample size is small relative to the population (<m>30/1000 = 0.03 \lt 0.05</m>), the probabilities you calculated earlier quite similar! It is therefore reasonable to just fix the probability of success at <m>p = \frac{65}{1000} = 0.065</m> for all 30 trials. This would allow us to use the binomial distribution to get an <em>approximation</em> for <m>P(X=3)</m>! 
            </p>
            <p>
                 Re-compute the probability that exactly 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged using the binomial approximation. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three places. 
                 
                 </p>
                  <p>
                  How does this compare to your initial answer using the hypergeometric distribution in Part A? 
            </p>
         <p>
           <fillin width="8" answer="0.182"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the Binomial formula:
                <me>P(X=3) = \binom{30}{3}(0.065)^3(0.935)^{27} \approx 0.182</me>
                Comparing this to the hypergeometric result (<m>0.184</m>), the binomial approximation is incredibly close and accurate to within two-thousandths.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </task>
        </exercise>




<exercise label="ex-cookie-analysis">
        <title>Analyzing the Cookie Problem Approximation</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Refer back to the Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip cookie problem. You originally used a hypergeometric framework because you sampled a total of 3 cookies from a small batch of 21 cookies (where 8 were Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip flavors).
            </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                 Comment on whether a binomial approximation is mathematically appropriate for that cookie scenario. Justify your reasoning.
                </p>
              </statement>
            
        <solution>
            <p>
                The binomial approximation is <strong>not appropriate</strong> here. Testing the population threshold: <m>\frac{n}{N} = \frac{3}{21} \approx 0.143</m>. Because <m>14.3\% \gt 5\%</m>, the sample size is too large relative to the population. Each draw alters the remaining cookie composition too dramatically to ignore.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Regardless of appropriateness, let's see how much error occurs if we force a binomial calculation anyway. Let the constant probability of picking a Brussels-Sprout cookie be <m>p = \frac{8}{21} \approx 0.38</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                 Use the binomial distribution to compute the probability of getting exactly one Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip cookie in your sample of 3 cookies.
            </p>
        </statement>
         <solution>
            <p>
               Using the Binomial formula:P(X=1) = \binom{3}{1}(0.38)^1(0.62)^2 \approx 0.438
            </p>
        </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compare your answers using the hypergeometric and binomial distributions. What do you notice? Why did this occur?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              The exact hypergeometric result was 0.469. Because the sample size threshold was violated, the approximation is noticeably inaccurate (off by roughly 0.031 or over 3%).
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
       
</exercise>







        </exercises>

    


